1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,576
Green slime on the water tank.

2
00:00:02,576 --> 00:00:04,853
Velvet moss on the compound wall.

3
00:00:04,853 --> 00:00:06,677
A tall tree by the road.

4
00:00:07,077 --> 00:00:08,545
All three are plants.

5
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:13,028
But the slime lives wet and low,
and the tree stands tall in dry air.

6
00:00:13,478 --> 00:00:17,823
Diya asked: from water slime to
tall trees, what actually changed?

7
00:00:18,173 --> 00:00:19,344
Not one thing.

8
00:00:19,344 --> 00:00:23,540
Four things changed, step by
step, across five plant groups.

9
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:28,727
By the end, you will follow those four
trends from algae to flowering plants,

10
00:00:28,727 --> 00:00:31,269
and see why each step mattered on land.

11
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:34,896
First, the five groups,
from simple to complex.

12
00:00:35,246 --> 00:00:38,228
Algae: simple green
bodies, mostly in water.

13
00:00:38,228 --> 00:00:40,705
Bryophytes: the mosses and liverworts.

14
00:00:40,705 --> 00:00:43,580
Pteridophytes: the ferns
and their relatives.

15
00:00:44,030 --> 00:00:47,430
Gymnosperms: plants with
naked seeds, like pines.

16
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:51,455
Angiosperms: the flowering
plants, with seeds inside fruits.

17
00:00:51,905 --> 00:00:56,129
Now watch four things change along
this line: the body, the pipes, the

18
00:00:56,129 --> 00:00:58,610
dominant phase, and the need for water.

19
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:01,541
Let's look at one plant from each group.

20
00:01:01,891 --> 00:01:06,437
An alga is a thallus: a body with
no true roots, stem or leaves.

21
00:01:06,937 --> 00:01:08,940
A moss holds on with rhizoids.

22
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:13,145
It looks leafy, but it still has
no true roots, stems or leaves.

23
00:01:13,645 --> 00:01:16,622
A fern has true roots, a stem and leaves.

24
00:01:16,622 --> 00:01:21,199
Inside run xylem and phloem, the
first vascular tissue: pipes for water

25
00:01:21,199 --> 00:01:22,099
and food.

26
00:01:22,529 --> 00:01:25,293
A pine carries naked seeds on its cones.

27
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:28,572
A flowering plant packs
its seeds inside a fruit.

28
00:01:29,222 --> 00:01:32,790
Now let's run the tracker along
the line, one group at a time.

29
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:34,040
Algae.

30
00:01:33,784 --> 00:01:37,425
In most algae, the haploid
phase is the main body.

31
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:38,881
No vascular tissue.

32
00:01:38,881 --> 00:01:40,833
The gametes meet in water.

33
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:42,483
Bryophytes.

34
00:01:42,435 --> 00:01:45,534
The haploid gametophyte
is still the main plant.

35
00:01:45,534 --> 00:01:47,257
Still no vascular tissue.

36
00:01:47,257 --> 00:01:49,999
The male gametes still swim through water.

37
00:01:50,749 --> 00:01:51,773
Pteridophytes.

38
00:01:51,773 --> 00:01:52,705
The switch.

39
00:01:52,705 --> 00:01:57,123
Now the diploid sporophyte is the
main plant, with vascular tissue.

40
00:01:57,123 --> 00:02:01,065
But its small gametophyte still
needs water for fertilisation.

41
00:02:01,815 --> 00:02:02,740
Gymnosperms.

42
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:06,227
The gametophytes shrink,
and stay on the sporophyte.

43
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:11,188
Pollen travels on air currents, and a
pollen tube carries the male gametes.

44
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:12,438
No water needed.

45
00:02:13,188 --> 00:02:14,133
Angiosperms.

46
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:19,343
The same freedom from water, plus flowers,
and seeds protected inside fruits.

47
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:23,280
One more step hides between
ferns and seed plants.

48
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:26,700
Most pteridophytes make one kind of spore.

49
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:31,533
But Selaginella and Salvinia make
two kinds: large megaspores and small

50
00:02:31,533 --> 00:02:32,542
microspores.

51
00:02:33,292 --> 00:02:37,783
A megaspore grows into a female
gametophyte, and the young embryo starts

52
00:02:37,783 --> 00:02:39,330
to develop inside it.

53
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:44,448
This is a precursor to the seed
habit, an important step in evolution.

54
00:02:44,448 --> 00:02:48,468
In seed plants, the female
gametophyte stays on the parent plant.

55
00:02:49,218 --> 00:02:53,315
Now the hardest pair to tell apart:
bryophytes and pteridophytes.

56
00:02:53,665 --> 00:02:56,169
Both need water for fertilisation.

57
00:02:56,169 --> 00:02:57,939
Both spread by spores.

58
00:02:57,939 --> 00:03:00,499
Both have two multicellular phases.

59
00:03:00,949 --> 00:03:04,754
But a moss is mainly gametophyte,
with no vascular tissue.

60
00:03:04,754 --> 00:03:08,153
A fern is mainly sporophyte,
with xylem and phloem.

61
00:03:08,803 --> 00:03:09,703
Your turn.

62
00:03:09,646 --> 00:03:14,249
Which one can grow taller, and carry
water up a stem: the moss, or the

63
00:03:14,249 --> 00:03:15,149
fern?

64
00:03:14,791 --> 00:03:16,031
Pause and decide.

65
00:03:19,231 --> 00:03:20,131
The fern!

66
00:03:20,012 --> 00:03:23,346
Its xylem carries water
up, so it can stand taller.

67
00:03:23,346 --> 00:03:27,022
A moss without pipes stays
low, close to the damp ground.

68
00:03:27,672 --> 00:03:28,741
Remember this.

69
00:03:28,741 --> 00:03:33,744
Along the line, the sporophyte grows, the
gametophyte shrinks, and fertilisation

70
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:35,172
stops needing water.

71
00:03:35,772 --> 00:03:37,312
Now, a worked example.

72
00:03:37,312 --> 00:03:39,846
Let's match four clues to the right group.

73
00:03:40,196 --> 00:03:44,375
First vascular tissue, but water
still needed for fertilisation.

74
00:03:44,375 --> 00:03:45,939
That is pteridophytes.

75
00:03:46,389 --> 00:03:50,172
Main plant body haploid, with
the sporophyte attached to it.

76
00:03:50,172 --> 00:03:51,493
That is bryophytes.

77
00:03:51,943 --> 00:03:54,896
Naked seeds, and pollen carried by air.

78
00:03:54,896 --> 00:03:56,448
That is gymnosperms.

79
00:03:56,898 --> 00:03:59,117
Seeds enclosed inside fruits.

80
00:03:59,117 --> 00:04:00,754
That is angiosperms.

81
00:04:01,404 --> 00:04:02,869
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:04:03,219 --> 00:04:07,444
Many students think bryophytes were
the first plants with vascular tissue,

83
00:04:07,444 --> 00:04:09,177
because they live on land.

84
00:04:09,577 --> 00:04:12,221
But bryophytes have no xylem or phloem.

85
00:04:12,221 --> 00:04:16,098
Pteridophytes are the first land
plants with vascular tissue.

86
00:04:16,748 --> 00:04:18,164
Where is this asked?

87
00:04:18,164 --> 00:04:22,605
As statements: which group first had
vascular tissue, or which still needs

88
00:04:22,605 --> 00:04:24,155
water for fertilisation.

89
00:04:24,755 --> 00:04:26,025
Exam level now.

90
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:28,513
Read the assertion and the reason.

91
00:04:28,513 --> 00:04:29,838
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:35,238 --> 00:04:36,516
The answer is A.

93
00:04:36,516 --> 00:04:40,024
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

94
00:04:40,524 --> 00:04:44,277
Gymnosperm pollen is carried
by air currents to the ovule.

95
00:04:44,777 --> 00:04:48,966
Then a pollen tube carries the male
gametes to the egg, so they never have

96
00:04:48,966 --> 00:04:49,866
to swim.

97
00:04:50,245 --> 00:04:51,706
Here is your recap.

98
00:04:51,706 --> 00:04:53,207
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:56,407 --> 00:04:59,244
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

100
00:04:59,644 --> 00:05:00,544
One.

101
00:05:00,166 --> 00:05:02,739
Which group first had vascular tissue?

102
00:05:03,639 --> 00:05:04,539
Two.

103
00:05:04,167 --> 00:05:08,859
In which group is the haploid
gametophyte the main plant body on land?

104
00:05:09,759 --> 00:05:10,659
Three.

105
00:05:10,364 --> 00:05:14,046
Why was making two kinds of
spores a step towards seeds?

106
00:05:15,146 --> 00:05:19,543
Next time: a sponge, a jellyfish
and a cockroach on one page.

107
00:05:19,543 --> 00:05:21,314
How do we sort animals?

108
00:05:21,314 --> 00:05:24,470
We find out in basis of
animal classification.
